## Phase 3: Hierarchical Tiling and Pipeline Exploration

## Candidate 21: 3-level K tiling (L2=256 + L1=64) + [4,8,16] + vectorize + unroll_K×4
- Hypothesis: A_tile(L2=256)=128KB + B_tile(L2=256)=256KB → both fit L2
- Result: 1.503 ms — essentially SAME as C14 (no improvement from 3-level K)

## Candidate 22: Custom bufferization pipeline — innerreduction + full_unroll=false
- Changed: lower_multi_reduction from "innerparallel"→"innerreduction", transfer_to_scf full_unroll=false
- Result: 3.700 ms — MUCH WORSE
- Also tried: innerparallel + full_unroll=false → 3.948 ms — MUCH WORSE
- Key learning: default innerparallel + full_unroll=true is critical — LLVM auto-vectorizes from scalar code

## Candidate 23: N_L2=64 tile (reduce B tile from 64x128 to 64x64 = 32KB for L1)
- Schedule: forall[4,8] + N_L2[64] + K[64] + [4,8,16] + vectorize + unroll_K×4
- Result: 1.830 ms — WORSE
- Root cause: A matrix gets read TWICE (once per N_L2 iteration), negating cache benefit

## Candidate 24: [8,4,16] inner tile (wider M, narrower N)
- vector<8x4x16xf64> = 512 elements — AT LIMIT
- Result: 1.961 ms — WORSE ([4,8,16] M-N balance is optimal)

## Candidate 25: [4,8,8] + unroll_K×8 (more unrolled copies, smaller vector)
- vector<4x8x8xf64> = 256 elements, 8 unrolled K copies
- Result: 1.525 ms — close but slightly worse than C14

## Candidate 26: [4,8,16] + unroll_K×2 (fewer unroll copies)
- Result: 2.395 ms — WORSE (factor=4 is optimal, not 2)

## Candidate 27: pre-vectorize canonicalization (to force in_bounds=true)
- Added tiling_canonicalization + scf canonicalization BEFORE vectorize
- Result: 1.504 ms — SAME as C14 (in_bounds flags don't affect lowering path)

## Candidate 28: [4,8,16] + unroll_K×4 + unroll_kloop(outer)×2
- Combined inner+outer K unrolling to expose independent compute streams
- Result: 1.682 ms — WORSE (code bloat + register pressure from outer K unroll)

